WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.412
Pre-aggregation is one of the clearest ways to protect a higher-level grain.

00:00:04.412 --> 00:00:09.682
Instead of joining raw admissions, raw lab results, and raw prescriptions to patients,

00:00:09.682 --> 00:00:13.492
we first summarize each child table by patient ID.

00:00:13.492 --> 00:00:17.592
The admission summary produces at most one row per patient.

00:00:17.592 --> 00:00:21.397
The lab summary produces at most one row per patient.

00:00:21.397 --> 00:00:23.832
The prescription summary does the same.

00:00:23.832 --> 00:00:28.262
Now the three child sources are already at patient grain.

00:00:28.262 --> 00:00:34.782
When we join those summaries to the patients table, the two-times-two-times-two explosion cannot occur.

00:00:34.782 --> 00:00:39.292
The query uses common table expressions to create the three summaries.

00:00:39.292 --> 00:00:45.642
Then it LEFT JOINs each summary to patients so patients with no activity are still preserved.

00:00:45.642 --> 00:00:49.537
COALESCE converts missing counts from NULL to zero.

00:00:49.537 --> 00:00:53.082
This pattern is common in dashboards and feature tables.

00:00:53.082 --> 00:00:57.797
A customer-level dashboard might summarize orders, support tickets,

00:00:57.797 --> 00:01:00.722
and refunds separately before combining them.

00:01:00.722 --> 00:01:07.602
A student-level dataset might summarize registrations, submissions, and advising visits separately.

00:01:07.602 --> 00:01:13.377
The key idea is to make each source compatible with the target grain before combining the sources.
